# IOCL bill automation — credentials and working data (never commit)
tools/iocl_recon/gmail_credentials.json
tools/iocl_recon/gmail_token.json
# Per-mailbox tokens (jaiswal_token.json, ...). Glob, not one line per file:
# these are live credentials and a new profile must not be committable just
# because nobody remembered to add a line here for it.
tools/iocl_recon/*_token.json
tools/iocl_recon/*_credentials.json
tools/iocl_recon/__pycache__/
tools/fuel_recon/__pycache__/
tools/loan_recon/__pycache__/
uploads/iocl_bills/
reports/iocl_recon/
# Parsed fuel-pump rows: real litres, rates and vehicle numbers from 11 pumps'
# bills. Business data, same rule as uploads/ and the PG dumps — the PARSER is
# tracked, its output is not.
reports/fuel_rows.json
reports/fuel_recon/
reports/loan_bills/tata_ledgers.json
reports/fuel_rows_recovery.json
# Hand-read scanned pump bills. ocr_bands/ is page images of the bills
# themselves — the commercial paperwork, same rule as uploads/. ocr_bills/ and
# ocr_rows.json are what a reader made of them: every litre, rate, registration
# and rupee off 30 real invoices. fleet_norms.json is the truck list they were
# matched against. All business data; the TOOLS that produce it are tracked.
reports/ocr_bands/
reports/ocr_bills/
reports/ocr_rows.json
reports/fleet_norms.json
# Toll read off the banks' FASTag statements: every plaza crossing with the
# lorry number, the minute and the rupee. A movement log of the whole fleet.
reports/toll_rows.json
reports/toll_stage.csv
# Loan contracts read off the financiers' statements: account numbers, chassis
# numbers, borrower and guarantor names, and the amount owed on every truck.
reports/loan_contracts.json
reports/loan_bills/
reports/loan_bands/
reports/_loan_probe.json

# 📄 Scanned business documents (IOCL advices, bills, scans) — real commercial
# paperwork, 400+ files. Only iocl_bills/ was excluded before, which let the
# advices and scans through. Whole directory stays local.
uploads/
# ...and any copy of it. A backup of an ignored directory is the same documents
# under a name the rule above does not match, which is exactly how they get
# committed by an `add -A`.
uploads.*.bak/

# 🗄️ PostgreSQL dumps — a dump is the company's entire book of account. Once in
# git history it cannot be removed without rewriting it, so backups live outside
# the repo. The docs and nginx config in deploy/aws/sync/ ARE tracked.
*.dump
*.sql.gz

# 📊 Generated slide deck (rebuild with: node scripts/render-slides.cjs)
Prasad_Transport_Presentation_2026.pdf

# 🔑 Android release signing. The upload key is what Google Play uses to prove
# an update really came from us — in git it is a published private key. The
# keystore lives in F:\Prasad_Transport_Data\keystore\ with its own credentials
# note; this file only carries the path and passwords.
android/keystore.properties
*.jks
*.keystore

# 📦 Android build output (the .aab is exported to F:\Prasad_Transport_Data\builds)
android/app/build/
android/build/
android/.gradle/
android/app/src/main/assets/public/
scripts/drive-separation-*.log

# reports/ is a junction to F:\Prasad_Transport_Data\reports — generated output
# on the data volume, not source. It was never ignored, so a `git add -A` would
# have pulled 26 MB of generated reports into the code history.
reports/

# Runtime logs written INTO the repo by a writer that ignored LOG_DIR.
# 22 MB of them accumulated by 20-08-2026. This is the failure CLAUDE.md
# describes: a writer hardcodes a repo-relative path, survives the drive move
# unnoticed, and leaves a second live log directory nobody is watching. Ignored
# so it can never reach git or a deploy; the writer itself still needs fixing.
Prasad_Transport_Datalogs/
